/** @page grpclimitsguide Message and Body Size Limits

    @tableofcontents

    By default neither the server nor the client limits the size of the data it receives.  The server limits the
    request data it holds in memory for a call with two
    @ref Qore::Grpc::GrpcServerOptions "GrpcServerOptions":
    - \c max_message_size: the maximum size in bytes of one request message, checked against the length declared
      in the message's gRPC frame before the message is buffered
    - \c max_request_body_size: the maximum size in bytes of a request body held in memory; it is also the
      \c max_request_body_size of the server's HTTP/2 listeners, which limits the request data buffered for a
      stream that has not yet been read.  The body of a unary or server-streaming call, which is read completely
      before the handler is called, is limited as it is read.  The body of a client-streaming or bidirectional call
      is read one message at a time, so only its messages are limited, by \c max_message_size

    A call that exceeds a limit fails with @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED, and the rest of its
    request is discarded.  A unary or server-streaming handler is not called.  In a client-streaming or bidirectional
    handler, @ref Grpc::GrpcServerStream::read() "GrpcServerStream::read()" raises \c GRPC-RESOURCE-EXHAUSTED (a
    message over \c max_message_size) or \c HTTP-BODY-TOO-LARGE (the listener's limit), and the stream returns no
    further messages; if the handler does not catch the exception, the client receives
    @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED.

    @code{.py}
GrpcServer server(schema, <GrpcServerOptions>{
    "max_message_size": 4 * 1024 * 1024,        # 4 MiB per request message
    "max_request_body_size": 8 * 1024 * 1024,   # 8 MiB per request body
});
    @endcode

    The client limits the response data it holds in memory for a call with two
    @ref Qore::Grpc::GrpcChannelOptions "GrpcChannelOptions", which the \c grpc:// and \c flight:// connections and
    the \c GrpcDataProvider and \c ArrowFlightDataProvider constructor options also accept:
    - \c max_message_size: the maximum size in bytes of one response message, checked against the length declared
      in the message's gRPC frame before the message is buffered
    - \c max_response_body_size: the maximum size in bytes of the response body of a unary call, which is received
      completely before it is decoded; it is the \c max_response_body_size of the channel's
      \c HttpClientConnectionManager.  The response of a streaming
      call is received one message at a time, so only its messages are limited, by \c max_message_size

    A unary call that exceeds a limit returns @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED.  A stream that receives
    a message over \c max_message_size is cancelled, so the server stops sending, and ends with
    @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED "GRPC_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED": @ref Grpc::GrpcClientStream::read() "GrpcClientStream::read()"
    raises \c GRPC-RESOURCE-EXHAUSTED and @ref Grpc::GrpcClientStream::finish() "finish()" returns the status, and a
    @ref Grpc::GrpcAsyncClientStream "GrpcAsyncClientStream" delivers it as its result.

    @code{.py}
GrpcChannel channel("https://api.example.com:443", <GrpcChannelOptions>{
    "max_message_size": 4 * 1024 * 1024,        # 4 MiB per response message
    "max_response_body_size": 8 * 1024 * 1024,  # 8 MiB per unary response body
});
    @endcode
*/
